#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193748} :activity/id #uuid "0f7a1ce6-56ee-5c6f-be6e-f0a5c31de7ad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048654 :topic/id #uuid "b28606bf-b94d-5f84-9c4e-056d3eee9687" :topic/math-academy-id 3946} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193806 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be an $n \\times m$ observation matrix with standardized data, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature.\n\nSuppose we have the following **singular value decomposition** (SVD) of $X{:}$ \n\n$$\nX = U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nNote that in the SVD, the columns of $U$ are orthonormal, which implies that $U^T U = I$ (the identity matrix). \n\nUsing this decomposition, the sample covariance matrix of $X$ can be derived as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}{{({U}^{T}U)}_{⏟}}_{I}Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ \n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The matrix $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ is a diagonal $m \\times m$ matrix with the eigenvalues of $C$ (in descending order) on the main diagonal.\n\n- The matrix $V$ is an $m \\times m$ matrix whose columns are the unit eigenvectors of $C.$\n\nTo summarize, if we write down the singular value decomposition of the standardized observation matrix $X,$ the columns of the matrix $V$ in the SVD are the **principal components** of the data." :tutorial/id #uuid "fec0f21a-271c-5558-9427-a50d62b4e629" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4fcc5fda-8a47-5812-b960-71863e23d4e9" :step/math-academy-id 610887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192612 :question/id #uuid "b0a1a305-4617-50f6-a0d9-2b573277f68f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21883"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049975}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526064}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"} :step/id #uuid "15ca33ca-5d01-5308-bba1-0c17a2514826" :step/math-academy-id 610888 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193807 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a sample of $n$ observations, where each observation has $m$ features. We store this data in the $n \\times m$ observation matrix $X,$ where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature.\n\n![](images/fe/fe9bd930e502d38eccc99f9c9a7a4b994bfa6caed26dd0b2a2675128ef18252a.svg)\n\nWe assume the data has been mean-centered. Let $\\mathbf{x}_j$ denote the $j$th column of $X.$ The sample covariance matrix, $C_X,$ is given by\n\n![](images/12/126db3a0c2d3fde367318bab5f59bccae7dd2cab8342435e7faf7de24e690997.svg)\n\nThe **total variance** of the observed data equals the sum of the entries on the leading diagonal of $C_X$ (the trace of the matrix). That is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Variance}  = \\textrm{Var}[\\mathbf{x}_1] + \\textrm{Var}[\\mathbf{x}_2] + \\cdots + \\textrm{Var}[\\mathbf{x}_m].\n$$\n\nHow is the total variance related to the singular values of $X?$\n\nWe recall that using the singular value decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T,$ the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1,\\ldots, \\lambda_m$ of $C_X$ are related to the singular values $\\sigma_1,\\ldots, \\sigma_m$ of $X$ by:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_i = \\dfrac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1} \\quad \\text{for } i=1,\\ldots,m.\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we will use this relationship to compute the total variance." :tutorial/id #uuid "b4ae7cae-c19f-5ca8-9b4f-42f746d53a9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14787 :tutorial/title "Total Variance of the Observation Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9f54348f-13f4-5af1-8c13-0e7909839edf" :step/math-academy-id 610891 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193808 :tutorial/content "A nutritionist measured $3$ features among a group of clients:\n\n- body mass index ($x_1$)\n\n- height ($x_2$)\n\n- weight ($x_3$)\n\nThe collected data was stored in the mean-centered observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client. The nutritionist finds that the singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=12, \\qquad \\sigma_2=5, \\qquad \\sigma_3=4.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then decides to reduce the dimensionality of the data set. To do this, they convert the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation \n\n$$\nY = X V_1,\n$$\n\nwhere $V_1$ is the $3 \\times 1$ matrix containing the first principal component of the data only. As a result, $Y$ is an $n \\times 1$ matrix that gives a single feature associated with each client.\n\nWhat percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$\n\nThe total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =185.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first principal component, the preserved variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Preserved} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Preserved}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{185} \\\\  & ≈0.778 \\\\  & =77.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- The nutritionist reduced the dimensionality of the data from $3$ (initially, each client had three features) to just $1$ (only one feature characterizes each client after the transformation).\n\n- In doing so, $77.8\\%$ of the variance is preserved in the transformed data." :tutorial/id #uuid "bde3e2b4-d644-5955-bbda-5e4e79c40df2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14788 :tutorial/title "Computing the Remaining Variance After Dimensionality Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "c2cc97eb-c337-5109-9c6d-41adae21e68d" :step/math-academy-id 610892 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192613 :question/id #uuid "ad59f3c2-8ca8-5d05-b626-501682800f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-21884"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526092}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"} :step/id #uuid "eca9251a-22f4-5c77-b3b2-ef3bbb2d347c" :step/math-academy-id 610893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193809 :tutorial/content "To find the total variance in terms of singular values, define the change of variables $Y = XV.$ The covariance of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{Y}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{Y}^{T}Y=\\frac{1}{n-1}(XV)^{T}(XV)={V}^{T}{{(\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X)}_{⏟}}_{{C}_{X}}V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $V$ contains the eigenvectors of $C_X,$ the matrix $C_Y$ is diagonal and contains the eigenvalues $\\lambda_i$ of $C_X{:}$\n\n![](images/c3/c344eb83c1e16eba7fbb71c00ce28dc72875826b5c487a33cd2235499c94d4f3.svg)\n\nThe total variance is defined as the trace (sum of diagonal elements) of the covariance matrix. A key property of the trace is that it is invariant under orthogonal transformations like $Y=XV.$ Therefore, the total variance of $Y$ is the same as the total variance of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Variance} = \\text{Tr}(C_X) = \\text{Tr}(C_Y) = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\lambda_i.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\lambda_i = \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1},$ we conclude that the total variance is *proportional to* the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{Total Variance} \\:\\propto\\: \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sigma^2_i.}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ab13b86-9d0f-53e4-b7b1-bee88604c031" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14956 :tutorial/title "Finding the Total Variance"} :step/id #uuid "aecacf05-e50b-52b9-85aa-b28cf5b31b8c" :step/math-academy-id 611564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193751}}] :activity/title "The Connection Between PCA and SVD" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192866}]}
